        "The Gothic tower facing the Midway is International House, and under it lies the hotel this tour has already mentioned twice. In April 1893, with the fair about to open, a buff brick hotel called the **Raymond and Whitcomb Grand** was rushed to completion right here. Four stories, 325 rooms, another 62 in an annex, and its best feature ran the full length of the building. A **250-foot verandah facing the Midway**, so guests could take breakfast watching the strangest street on earth. It soon took a grander name, the Del Prado.",
        "The fair minted hotels like this all along the neighborhood's edges. The Del Prado, the Chicago Beach, the Windermere, all opened for 1893 with wonders the guests wrote home about. Hot and cold running water, steam heat, electric light, telephones. The Illinois Central raised its tracks onto the embankment you crossed earlier just to move the crowds, an estimated four million riders in one summer. When the fair ended, the hotels stayed, and for a generation this stretch ran as one of the most famous resort hotel districts in the country.",
        "It did not last. By 1930 the Del Prado was, in one writer's words, worn and tired, its great verandah long since enclosed. The university bought the ground, pulled the hotel down, and raised **International House**, a residence built with Rockefeller money to board students from around the world. Its name moved up to 53rd Street, where the Cooper-Carlton wears it still.",
        "Notice what happened on this block, because it happens again and again from here on. A neighborhood institution ages, the university buys the land, and something in Gothic stone replaces it. In the decades ahead the university would absorb the Piccadilly, the Broadview, and the Shoreland the same way. There is a real irony in the particular swap made here. The hotel had been a private pleasure palace for white travelers. The house that replaced it was built so students from around the world could live under one roof, in a neighborhood that was at that very moment sealing itself shut against Black Chicagoans a mile west. Both things are true on this ground. Hold them both, and walk on up the Midway.",
