      id: "robie-house",
      number: 8,
      title: "The Robie House",
      dek: "What the professors' quarter built when money was no object",
      mapLabel: "Robie House",
      lat: 41.78985,
      lng: -87.59585,
      audioSrc: `${MEDIA}/audio/stop-08.mp3`,
      audioSeconds: 157,
      transcript: [
        "Frederick Robie made bicycle parts. He ran his father's supply company, he was twenty-eight, and in 1908 he went to **Frank Lloyd Wright** with an engineer's brief. No boxy rooms, no drafts, and he wanted to see his neighbors without his neighbors seeing him. By 1910 he had this. Long Roman brick, roofs that hang far past their walls, a house that seems to be gliding while standing still. Wright called the style Prairie. Plenty of the neighbors just called it strange.",
        "For this walk, though, the house is really about money, and about the street it sits on. When the university opened in 1892, the blocks around it filled with big houses for professors, deans, and executives, many built on lots bought from Marshall Field himself. Woodlawn Avenue became the street where the money showed. Walk a block in either direction after this stop and look at the porches and the stonework. Hiring the most radical architect in America was simply what this street could afford, and it is exactly the class of resident Paul Cornell had imagined from the beginning.",
        "The house itself was nearly lost twice. The seminary next door announced demolition in 1941, and a letter campaign saved it. It tried again in 1957, and this time the rescue came from an unexpected direction. Wright, ninety years old, declared it the finest house he ever designed, Carl Sandburg compared the wreckers to book burners, and the man who actually wrote the check was **William Zeckendorf, the developer then building the new 55th Street on the urban renewal land**. He bought the house, used it as his site office, and passed it to the university. The man profiting most from the neighborhood's demolition saved its most famous house. Today it is a World Heritage Site.",
        "Now hold the two halves of this block in your head at once, because this is the turn in the whole story. Money confident enough to build this house was also confident enough to write rules about who could never own one. In 1909, one year after Robie hired Wright, a Hyde Park lawyer founded an improvement club whose stated purpose was keeping Black families out. Within a generation, the real estate profession had made that project official ethics. The red plate below is the rulebook itself.",
      ],
      interrupts: [
        {
          title: "The improvement associations",
          body: [
            "An improvement association was **a homeowners' club organized to control who could live on its blocks**. The clubs came before the paperwork, and this neighborhood built the prototype. In **August 1909** a Hyde Park attorney named Francis Harper organized the **Hyde Park Improvement Protective Club**, 350 members strong, with a stated mission of maintaining the color line. Its targets were not newcomers. Black doctors, teachers, and shopkeepers had lived in Hyde Park for fifteen years. The club pledged to **blacklist any realtor who sold to a Black buyer**, to buy out Black homeowners, and to replace Black workers with whites. A decade on, the neighborhood's Property Owners' Journal was still putting the theory plainly. **\"Every colored man who moves into Hyde Park knows that he is damaging his white neighbors' property. Therefore, he is making war on the white man.\"** The educator Fannie Barrier Williams, a Hyde Parker of fifteen years, answered for her neighbors. \"I've lived here for 15 years. What you trying to drive me out of my community?\"",
            "Where the paper failed, dynamite followed. Between **1917 and 1921 white Chicagoans bombed more than fifty Black homes** and the offices of the Black banker **Jesse Binga**, who kept writing mortgages anyway and told the papers, \"If they can make me move, they will say, we made Jesse Binga move. No, I will not run.\" Of the forty bombed families, two moved. In 1918 Baltimore's mayor wrote his realty board about \"a plan they have\" in Chicago for keeping Black families out of white blocks. Around the country, the association method became known simply as **the Chicago plan**.",
            "Then the profession absorbed it. In **1917 the Chicago Real Estate Board adopted block-by-block segregation as policy**, in **1921 it voted to expel any member who broke the line**, and in **1924 the national realtors' association wrote it into the code of ethics**. Article 34 read, \"A Realtor should never be instrumental in introducing into a neighborhood a character of property or occupancy, members of any race or nationality, or any individuals whose presence will clearly be detrimental to property values in that neighborhood.\" Selling a house to a Black family in a white neighborhood was, by the profession's own written standard, an ethics violation. **The race language stayed in the code until 1950.**",
          ],
        },
      ],
